37.m:
Pulastya said:
"Dwelling in Pātāla, O Brahmin, Andhaka was tormented by the fire of love;
his body scorched, he said this to all the Dānavas:

nor the fruit of sons, nor shall you attain union with a husband.’
As soon as the curse was uttered, thirteen [years] passed away;
and when the king was driven away, she too fell into a swoon.”

Likewise another, the daughter of a Guhyaka celebrated as Nandayantī,
the ascetic daughter of Añjana, will also arrive there;
and another, the auspicious Vedavatī, the daughter of Parjanya.

39.m:
Seeing Gālava, she stood up and saluted him;
and he, about to worship Mahādeva, saluted the great sages,
and the best of kings also paid homage to that treasure of austerity.

Rejoicing and happy, they remained enjoying their beloved pleasures.
Such indeed was the event concerning Citrāṅgadā in ancient times, O auspicious one;
therefore love me, O lotus-petal-eyed lady, O best of women!
